tion, beats everything.\n        13. Quadplex set: a quad with two single cards OR two pairs attached.\n        Use the EXACT card symbols provided in the Valid Moves list.\n        Prepare a valid JSON object like {"cards": ["♠3", "♥3"]} for your answer, along with your explanation and reason. If you pass, output: {"cards": []}\n        \n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output the action with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n    ', 'user_prompt': "You are playing as a peasant. Your aim is to be the play out all your cards before the landlord does, OR help your teammate (the other peasant) play out their cards before the landlord does. If the landlord plays out all cards first, you lose.\nYou are a Peasant. The Next Player (Right) is the Landlord (ENEMY). The Previous Player (Left) is your TEAMMATE (Peasant). You must cooperate with your teammate to beat the Landlord. The objective is to empty your hand before the landlord does, or help your teammate empty their hand before the landlord does.\n\nYour Hand: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♦4 ♥5 ♣10 ♣J ♦K ♥A ♣A ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\nLast Play to Beat: None (Free Play)\n--- Game History (Recent) ---\nTurn 4 (You): Pass\nTurn 5 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 5 (Prev Player): ♠6 ♣7 ♦8 ♠9 ♠10 ♥J ♣Q ♣K ♦A\nTurn 5 (You): Pass\nTurn 6 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 6 (Prev Player): ♣4\nTurn 6 (You): ♠5\nTurn 7 (Next Player): ♥Q\nTurn 7 (Prev Player): ♥2\nTurn 7 (You): Pass\nTurn 8 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 8 (Prev Player): ♥6\nTurn 8 (You): ♥9\nTurn 9 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 9 (Prev Player): Pass\n--- Player Info ---\nCards Held by Next Player (Right) [Landlord (Opponent)]: 4\nCards Held by Prev Player (Left) [Peasant (Teammate)]: 2\n--- Cards Remaining Unknown (Card Counting) ---\n(Format explanation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nQ: 1, 9: 1, 8: 2, 7: 2\nValid Moves:\npair: ♣3 ♦3\npair: ♥A ♣A\npair: ♣2 ♦2\nsingle: ♦3\nsingle: ♦4\nsingle: ♥5\nsingle: ♣10\nsingle: ♣J\nsingle: ♦K\nsingle: ♣A\nsingle: ♣2\ntriple: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3\ntriple: ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_pair: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♥A ♣A\ntriple_with_pair: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_pair: ♣3 ♦3 ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_pair: ♥A ♣A ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_single: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♦4\ntriple_with_single: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♥5\ntriple_with_single: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♣10\ntriple_with_single: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♣J\ntriple_with_single: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♦K\ntriple_with_single: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♣A\ntriple_with_single: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♣2\ntriple_with_single: ♦3 ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_single: ♦4 ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_single: ♥5 ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_single: ♣10 ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_single: ♣J ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_single: ♦K ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\ntriple_with_single: ♣A ♠2 ♣2 ♦2\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:  \nThis is a free play (no previous play to beat), so I can initiate any valid combination. My hand is very strong, especially with the triple 3s and two 2s that can be used in various combinations. My teammate (previous player) has only 2 cards left, meaning they are very close to winning. The landlord (next player) has 4 cards left. Therefore, my goal should be to avoid disrupting my teammate’s path to winning — ideally, I should either finish this turn by playing all my cards or play in a way that does not allow the landlord to gain control.\n\nKey observations:\n- My teammate passed last turn, indicating they likely cannot win immediately unless given a favorable follow-up.\n- Since they have only 2 cards left, those could be a pair or two singles. They likely need a single or a pair led for them to go out.\n- I have multiple powerful moves, including bombs (triples of 2s or 3s with attachments), but using them wisely is key.\n- Playing a **triple_with_single** or **triple_with_pair** might leave me with leftover cards, giving the landlord a chance to take control.\n- However, if I can finish now, I should.\n\nLooking at valid moves:\n- **Triple ♠3 ♣3 ♦3**: This clears early small cards and leaves behind higher ones, but doesn’t empty my hand.\n- **Triple_with_single**: e.g., ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 + one single — this uses four cards, still leaves many.\n- **Triple_with_pair**: e.g., ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♥A ♣A — uses five cards, still leaves several.\n- But notice: **triple_with_pair: ♠3 ♣3 ♦3 ♥A ♣A** uses 5 cards, leaving me with ♦4, ♥5, ♣10, ♣J, ♦K, ♠2, ♣2, ♦2 — still 8 cards.\n- Alternatively, **triple_with_pair: ♠2 ♣2 ♦2 + attached pair ♥A ♣A** is also valid — same structure.\n\nBut crucially: I do **not** have four 2s — only three (♠2, ♣2, ♦2). So no bomb from 2s. But triplet of 2s is possible.\n\nWait — I have **two viable bombs?** No: a bomb requires four of the same rank. I don’t have four of any rank. So **no bombs**. The triplet of 3s or 2s are just triplets, not bombs.\n\nSo the strongest move I can make now is a **triple_with_pair**, which cannot be beaten by anything except a rocket (unlikely) or a bomb (which would require four of a kind — possibly held by landlord? Unknown).\n\nBut more importantly: **my teammate has only 2 cards left**. If I play a **single card** or a **pair**, and the landlord cannot beat it, my teammate may be able to pass and let the landlord waste a move, or even win if the landlord plays something beatable.\n\nHowever, since this is a **free play**, I must play something — I cannot pass.\n\nMy best chance to help my teammate is to **avoid taking control unnecessarily** — but since I must play, I should play in a way that minimizes my remaining cards and maximizes pressure on the landlord.\n\nBut here\'s a better idea: **lead a single low card** to try to let my teammate go out.\n\nFor example:\n- If my teammate has two high cards (like two 2s or a 2 and an A), they might not benefit from a low single.\n- But earlier, they played ♣4 and then passed, and before that a long sequence ending in ♦A. So likely their remaining two cards are high.\n\nAlternatively, they might be holding two low cards to finish after a low lead.\n\nBut in Turn 6, they played ♣4 (a low card), and I responded with ♠5 — which was playable. Then landlord played ♥Q, teammate played ♥2 (very high), then I passed.\n\nThat suggests teammate used their **♥2** as a high escape — so perhaps they were forced to dump a 2 early, meaning their remaining two cards are not 2s.\n\nGiven that, and that they passed twice recently, it\'s possible they are waiting for a **single** or **pair** lead.\n\nNow, looking at my options:\n\nBest move to facilitate teammate:\n- Play a **single card** — such as ♦4 or ♥5 — low, so that if teammate has slightly higher card, they can win and play out.\n- But I have no information that they can beat even a 5 or 6.\n- Or play a **pair**, like ♣3♦3 — but I don\'t have a pair of 3s without breaking up the triplet.\n\nWait: valid moves include **pair: ♣3 ♦3** — yes, that’s listed. But I also have ♠3, so playing ♣3♦3 would leave ♠3 alone.\n\nBut if I play **pair: ♥A ♣A**, that’s a high pair — likely unbeatable unless landlord has a bomb or rocket. Landlord would probably pass, then teammate could only win if they have a higher pair (but no pair above A), so impossible. So that would end the round with me winning control — bad, because I still have many cards.\n\nSimilarly, playing a high single like ♦K or ♣A risks winning the trick, forcing me to continue — which I don’t want, since I have many cards.\n\nInstead, I should **play a low single** to give teammate a chance to overtake with a slightly higher card and potentially exit.\n\nI have:\n- ♦4 — lowest single available\n\nIf I play ♦4, and teammate has 5, 6, 7, etc., they could play it — but we know from card counting:\n- Q:1, 9:1, 8:2, 7:2 → these are unknown cards, could be anywhere.\n\nBut teammate had a long sequence earlier (up to ♦A), so likely dumped mid-cards already.\n\nStill, playing **♦4** gives the best chance for a smooth transition: low enough that if teammate has a mid-card, they can play it, and possibly continue.\n\nBut wait — after me comes the **landlord**, who acts before my teammate (order: me → landlord → teammate).\n\nSo sequence:\n- I play ♦4\n- Landlord can play any higher single (e.g., 5, 6, ..., 2) or pass\n- If landlord plays higher, teammate may not be able to respond\n- If landlord passes, teammate can play any higher single to win, then potentially play again next turn\n\nBut teammate only has 2 cards — so if they win this trick, they must play both cards in subsequent turns (if allowed).\n\nBut after winning a trick, you get to lead next.\n\nSo ideal path:\n- I play ♦4\n- Landlord passes (maybe they don’t want to waste a card)\n- Teammate plays, say, ♠5 or any higher single >4, wins trick\n- Now teammate leads — they can play one of their two cards, say a single\n- If landlord can’t follow, I might be able to cover, or they go out\n\nBut teammate only has 2 cards — they might not be able to win and then play two separate moves unless they have two singles and landlord keeps passing.\n\nAlternatively, if I play a **pair**, and teammate has a matching pair, they could beat it — but pairs are harder to match.\n\nBest shot: **play a low single** to enable teammate to win with minimal effort.\n\nAvailable low singles: ♦4, ♥5\n\n♦4 is lower than ♥5, so better to start with the lowest.\n\nSo **single: ♦4** is optimal.\n\nIt doesn’t risk me taking control (since it’s low, likely to be beaten or passed over), and gives teammate a chance to jump in.\n\nReason:  \nMy teammate is on 2 cards and very close to winning. By playing the lowest single possible (♦4), I maximize the chance that they can play a slightly higher card to win the trick. Even if the landlord intervenes, this still pressures them to use a card, while preserving my strong hand for future defense. Leading low supports cooperation; leading high or strong combos risks me taking control and wasting momentum.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦4"]}'}
